[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186061068 :answer-field/choices 17592186061072] [:db/retract 17592186061068 :answer-field/choices 17592186061073] [:db/retract 17592186061068 :answer-field/choices 17592186061069] [:db/retract 17592186061068 :answer-field/choices 17592186061070] [:db/retract 17592186061068 :answer-field/choices 17592186061071] {:db/id 17592186064473 :question/problem "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $2.2.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.2+3\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+0.4+0.3+0.8 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.5+0.8+0.9+3.2)-4 \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{1.4}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,0.028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2) & ≈P(Z>\\frac{2.2-2}{\\sqrt{0.028}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.20) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2)=1-Φ(1.20).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061068 :answer-field/correct "answer-4390c73f-e235-5d8c-b21e-b2ce65a311eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e3047eec-f665-516c-929d-83b012053579" "answer-57e71192-a1a2-5e8e-aef6-9944ca5ebd67" "answer-4390c73f-e235-5d8c-b21e-b2ce65a311eb" "answer-bc0cd694-3390-55f4-b3d4-294d1005a80d" "answer-4ee5e8c3-6e52-5c19-a9e8-1d052818d8ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3047eec-f665-516c-929d-83b012053579" :answer/id #uuid "e3047eec-f665-516c-929d-83b012053579" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.98)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57e71192-a1a2-5e8e-aef6-9944ca5ebd67" :answer/id #uuid "57e71192-a1a2-5e8e-aef6-9944ca5ebd67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.19)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4390c73f-e235-5d8c-b21e-b2ce65a311eb" :answer/id #uuid "4390c73f-e235-5d8c-b21e-b2ce65a311eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.20)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc0cd694-3390-55f4-b3d4-294d1005a80d" :answer/id #uuid "bc0cd694-3390-55f4-b3d4-294d1005a80d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.22)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ee5e8c3-6e52-5c19-a9e8-1d052818d8ca" :answer/id #uuid "4ee5e8c3-6e52-5c19-a9e8-1d052818d8ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.43)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e5110f55-9d1f-50ed-96e1-dbcf1735dc00" :activity/id #uuid "e5110f55-9d1f